text:
**Refined prompt:** Diagnose why an expired JWT produces `500` instead of `401` on this NestJS RealWorld API, answering from the code with file:line citations, and give the concrete fix — covering every path an expired token can crash on, not just the one the mobile app happened to hit.

## Why

`jsonwebtoken` throws a `TokenExpiredError` (a plain `Error`, not an `HttpException`) when the token's `exp` has passed. Nest's default exceptions filter maps `HttpException` to its status and everything else to `500 Internal Server Error`. There's no global filter to change that — `main.ts:5-21` never calls `useGlobalFilters`, and there's no `APP_FILTER` provider anywhere in `src`.

There are **two** unguarded `jwt.verify` call sites, and which one fires depends on the route:

**1. `src/user/auth.middleware.ts:17`** — the guard, on every protected route:

```ts
const decoded: any = jwt.verify(token, SECRET);   // ← no try/catch
```

Note the asymmetry: the two *other* failure modes right next to it are handled correctly — missing header → `401` (`auth.middleware.ts:28`), user id no longer exists → `401` (`auth.middleware.ts:21`). Only the verify throw escapes. That's why this reads as "auth works, the server is just broken."

**2. `src/user/user.decorator.ts:15`** — the optional-identity path on *public* routes:

```ts
if (token && token[1]) {
  const decoded: any = jwt.verify(token[1], SECRET);   // ← same, no try/catch
```

This one is the easier of the two to miss. On guarded routes the middleware throws first, so the decorator never runs. But `GET /profiles/:username` (`profile.controller.ts:19`) takes `@User('id')` while `ProfileModule.configure()` guards only `profiles/:username/follow` (`profile.module.ts:22`). So a public read that should degrade to anonymous also returns `500` when the app sends a stale token. `ArticleModule`'s eight `@User()` routes are all in its `forRoutes` list (`article.module.ts:24-31`), so they fail via site 1.

Contributing factor: tokens are signed with a 60-day expiry (`user.service.ts:104-113`) and there's no refresh endpoint, so every long-lived mobile install eventually walks into this.

## The fix

Wrap both, translating to `401`. This is exactly the rule LESSONS.md #79 records, and it covers both sites it names.

`src/user/auth.middleware.ts:17`:

```ts
let decoded: any;
try {
  decoded = jwt.verify(token, SECRET);
} catch (err) {
  throw new HttpException('Invalid token.', HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
}
const user = await this.userService.findById(decoded.id);
```

`src/user/user.decorator.ts:13-17`:

```ts
const token = req.headers.authorization ? (req.headers.authorization as string).split(' ') : null;
if (token && token[1]) {
  try {
    const decoded: any = jwt.verify(token[1], SECRET);
    return !!data ? decoded[data] : decoded.user;
  } catch (err) {
    throw new HttpException('Invalid token.', HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
  }
}
```

`HttpException`/`HttpStatus` are already imported in the middleware; the decorator needs them added from `@nestjs/common`.

One genuine judgement call, flagged rather than buried: on the *public* route, `401` versus silently treating the caller as anonymous. I've gone with `401` because that's what LESSONS.md #79 prescribes and because it's what actually gets the mobile app to clear its session — swallowing the error would leave the app holding a dead token with no signal to re-auth. The cost is that `GET /profiles/:username` starts rejecting a request it could have served anonymously.

Want me to apply both edits?

**What I did not read:** `user.service.ts` only around lines 99-126 (via grep context), not in full; and I did not run the app or the tests to reproduce the `500`.
